course: dictionary
lesson: 8287
title: Ripples that spread
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A ripple is a small wave, like the rings that spread out when you drop a stone in a pond. Ripple can also be a verb: «The flag rippled in the wind» means the flag moved in small waves.
explain: Use the context to choose the meaning. «Stop stroking a cat when its tail twitches, its skin ripples or its ears turn back.» Here the skin moves in small waves, which is a warning sign. Try your meaning in the sentence: "its skin moves in small waves" still makes sense.
explain: Ripple can also mean something spreading slowly, like «a ripple of laughter» moving through a classroom. The ripple effect works the same way: one small kind act can spread to more and more people.
fact: A ripple is a small wave on the surface of water.
fact: As a verb, ripple means to move in small waves: «The flag rippled in the wind.»
fact: «A ripple of applause» means applause spreading slowly through a crowd.
fact: The ripple effect means one small action causes more and more effects, like kindness spreading.
fact: In science, ripples show how waves spread out from a point.
exercise: «Stop stroking a cat when its skin ripples.» What does ripples mean here? | The cat falls asleep | The skin turns white | The skin is cut | *The skin moves in small waves
exercise: In «The flag rippled in the wind», what does rippled mean? | *Moved in small waves | Ran away fast | Stayed perfectly still | Shrank to nothing
exercise: «A ripple of laughter» suggests what? | *Laughter spreading slowly through a group | A loud noise from one person | Laughter that falls into a pond | A sudden stop to the laughter
test: «A trophic cascade is a change at the top of a food chain that ripples down.» What does ripples down mean here? | Falls straight to the ground | Stops at the top | *Spreads slowly down through the food chain | Sinks into the water
test: Why do scientists use the word ripples when talking about waves? | It is a word for very cold water | Ripples are only found in the sky | It means the waves are very large | *Ripples show how waves spread out from a point
exam: Which sentence uses ripple as a noun? | *Dropping a stone made a ripple on the pond | The cat will ripple its skin | Her laughter rippled across the room | We rippled the flag at dawn
